//? Title: Flood fill: exact regions, connectivity and actual stack work
//? Subtitle: Preserve the original grid, inspect native traversal evidence and check the complete input contract
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Clicking a paint bucket selects a connected region sharing the clicked cell's original
value. The replacement changes that region, while disconnected cells with the same
value remain unchanged. This report uses datamunge's iterative C++ flood fill, preserves
the original six-by-eight example and records actual discoveries, pops and neighbor
reads. A small diagonal example separates four from eight connectivity. Work counts,
shape validation and owned traces make the algorithm's behavior and costs explicit.
)

\toc

> The seed component and the actual traversal

Fix the seed's original integer value v. The selected region consists of all cells
reachable from the seed along a path whose cells all originally equal v, using the
chosen adjacency. Four connectivity uses up, down, left and right; eight also allows
diagonals. These are the usual image connectivity conventions described by
[Intel IPP](https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/docs/ipp/developer-guide-reference/2021-12/flood-fill-functions.html).
All signed integer values are valid categories. Zero is an ordinary value for flood
fill: seeding a zero can paint a background component too.

The native kernel keeps a heap-allocated LIFO stack. It paints a cell when pushing it,
so another neighbor cannot push it again. Four-direction scans run up/down/left/right;
eight-direction scans run NW/N/NE/W/E/SW/S/SE. LIFO means that a later discovery can
pop first. The pop order therefore describes depth-first work, not distance from the
seed, and need not match a recursive implementation's order.

The first program uses a two-by-two all-one grid. Its table and method diagram read
actual pop records from the executing kernel, rather than reconstructing an imagined
traversal. The first cell pushes down and then right, so right pops next even though
down was discovered first.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/flood_fill_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/label_images.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::flood_fill_trace({{1,1},{1,1}},0,0,9);
  std::print("| pop | row | column | pending after pop | pending after scan | probe range |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(54,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=3.5;style.height=2;style.font_size=23;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.visits.size();++i){const auto&v=t.visits[i];std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | [{},{}) |\n",i,v.row,v.col,v.stack_after_pop,v.stack_after_scan,v.probe_begin,v.probe_end);
    const auto id=std::to_string(i);d.node(id,{5.2*double(i),0},std::format("Pop {}: ({},{})\nPending {} → {}",i,v.row,v.col,v.stack_after_pop,v.stack_after_scan),style);if(i)d.edge(std::to_string(i-1),id);
  }
  const auto&w=t.work;std::print("\nChanged {}; direction slots {}; in-bounds value reads {}; neighbor discoveries {}; peak pending {}.\n",w.filled,w.neighbor_slots,w.in_bounds_neighbors,w.accepted_neighbors,w.peak_stack);
  d.text({7.8,-2.5},"Paint on push; each discovered coordinate pops once",25).save_svg("build/flood_fill_tiny.svg");
  if(w.filled!=4||w.peak_stack!=2||t.visits[1].col!=1)throw std::runtime_error("tiny actual traversal");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| pop | row | column | pending after pop | pending after scan | probe range |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 2 | [0,4) |
| 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 2 | [4,8) |
| 2 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | [8,12) |
| 3 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | [12,16) |

Changed 4; direction slots 16; in-bounds value reads 8; neighbor discoveries 3; peak pending 2.
\image(figures/flood_fill_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The actual four-pop order is top-left, top-right, bottom-right, bottom-left. Pending counts are stack occupancy, excluding the currently popped cell.)
\alttext(Four native nodes show actual coordinates and pending counts zero to two, one to two, one to one and zero to zero, joined in pop order.)

A cell is pushed and popped once, but it can be read by several neighbor probes.
Here sixteen direction slots include eight outside slots; eight slots actually read
grid values. Already painted queued cells can be observed before they pop. A
nonmatching value alone does not tell whether it was visited: the same replacement
value might have existed before the operation.

> Corner contact changes the selected region

The native checkerboard fixture has five one-valued cells in a three-by-three grid.
With four connectivity the corner seed has no one-valued orthogonal neighbor; with
eight connectivity the center joins all four corners. The following paired figure
uses a shared categorical palette, so value 9 means the same color in both panels.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/flood_fill_connectivity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/label_images.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto input=dm::algorithms::component_checkerboard(3,3);
  const auto four=dm::algorithms::flood_fill_trace(input,0,0,9,false);
  const auto eight=dm::algorithms::flood_fill_trace(input,0,0,9,true);
  std::print("| connectivity | changed | direction slots | peak pending | binary foreground components |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(bool diagonal:{false,true}){const auto&t=diagonal?eight:four;std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",diagonal?"Eight":"Four",t.work.filled,t.work.neighbor_slots,t.work.peak_stack,dm::algorithms::connected_components(input,diagonal).count);}
  dm::graphics::diagram_value_images({{four.output,"Four: one changed"},{eight.output,"Eight: five changed"}}).save_svg("build/flood_fill_connectivity.svg");
  if(four.work.filled!=1||eight.work.filled!=5)throw std::runtime_error("diagonal connectivity");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| connectivity | changed | direction slots | peak pending | binary foreground components |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Four | 1 | 4 | 1 | 5 |
| Eight | 5 | 40 | 3 | 1 |
\image(figures/flood_fill_connectivity.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Only the seed changes under four connectivity; corner paths through the center join five cells under eight connectivity. White cells retain zero.)
\alttext(Two three-by-three checkerboards show one nine on the left and five nines on the right. Unchanged ones and painted nines have distinct shared colors.)

The component count is a related binary control, not the flood-fill return value.
`connected_components` treats every nonzero cell as foreground, whereas flood fill
requires equality with one particular seed value. On [1,2] there is one binary
foreground component but a fill seeded at value 1 changes just one cell. Run labeling
on an exact-value mask if that is the region definition you need.

> Preserve the original six-by-eight example

The original fixture has two disconnected value-1 components, of sizes eight and five.
The seed is the upper-left cell, the replacement is 9, and connectivity is four.
The original before/after grids and changed count are printed below. Native component
statistics independently measure the two binary foreground areas before painting.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/flood_fill_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/label_images.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto input=dm::algorithms::flood_fill_example();auto output=input;
  const auto filled=dm::algorithms::flood_fill(output,0,0,9);
  for(bool after:{false,true}){std::print("\n{}:\n\n",after?"After":"Before");const auto&g=after?output:input;
    for(const auto&row:g){for(int v:row)std::print("{} ",v);std::print("\n");}}
  const auto stats=dm::algorithms::connected_component_stats(dm::algorithms::connected_components(input));
  std::print("\nOriginal fill: changed {}; foreground areas {} and {}; lower-right still one {}.\n",filled,stats[0].area,stats[1].area,output[3][5]==1);
  auto mixed=std::vector<std::vector<int>>{{1,2}};const auto components=dm::algorithms::connected_components(mixed).count;
  std::print("Mixed [1,2]: nonzero components {}; exact-value seed fill {}.\n",components,dm::algorithms::flood_fill(mixed,0,0,9));
  dm::graphics::diagram_value_images({{input,"Original"},{output,"After: eight cells painted"}}).save_svg("build/flood_fill_original.svg");
  if(filled!=8||stats[0].area!=8||stats[1].area!=5||output[3][5]!=1)throw std::runtime_error("original grid preservation");
}
```
