Original fill: changed 8; foreground areas 8 and 5; lower-right still one true.
Mixed [1,2]: nonzero components 1; exact-value seed fill 1.
\image(figures/flood_fill_original.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original eight-cell upper-left region changes from 1 to 9. All five cells of the lower-right value-1 region remain unchanged.)
\alttext(Two six-by-eight cell panels show the original two one-valued blobs and the upper-left blob painted nine. Background zero is white; numeric labels identify every cell.)

These colors are categories, not numerical intensities. `diagram_value_images`
assigns its eight-color cycle to the sorted union of nonzero values across the two
panels, keeping the same value consistent within a figure. More than eight categories
repeat colors, so numeric labels remain important. Signed values are supported;
zero is drawn white. The separate component-ID renderer keeps its historical cyclic
mapping, including the color alias between label IDs 1 and 9.

> Count work without substituting timings

Let R be the row count, N the total cells and K the number actually changed. Full
rectangular validation inspects every row width before any shortcut or mutation.
After that, each discovered cell changes value immediately, so it cannot be discovered
again. Every changed cell eventually pops, and each pop attempts exactly D neighbor
slots, with D=4 or 8. This proves total ordinary time O(R+K), rather than ignoring
shape validation in an O(K) claim. Work counts are actual integer events; they are
not hardware timings or a speed comparison with a queue or scanline method.

The pending stack needs O(K) space in the worst case. The explicit stack avoids
recursive call-depth limits but does not make the method constant-space. Its peak
depends on geometry and discovery order, even for equal region sizes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/flood_fill_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/label_images.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  using Grid=std::vector<std::vector<int>>;
  const std::vector<Grid> inputs{Grid(32,std::vector<int>(32,1)),Grid(1,std::vector<int>(1024,1)),dm::algorithms::component_checkerboard(32,32),dm::algorithms::component_checkerboard(32,32)};
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"Rectangle4","Strip4","Checker4","Checker8"};std::vector<double> slots,peaks;
  std::print("| input | rows | cells | changed K | direction slots | in-bounds reads | peak pending |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<inputs.size();++i){auto g=inputs[i];const auto w=dm::algorithms::flood_fill_work(g,0,0,9,i==3);std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",names[i],w.rows,w.cells,w.filled,w.neighbor_slots,w.in_bounds_neighbors,w.peak_stack);slots.push_back(double(w.neighbor_slots));peaks.push_back(double(w.peak_stack));if(w.neighbor_slots!=(i==3?8:4)*w.filled)throw std::runtime_error("direction work identity");}
  auto attempts=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(slots,names).title("Actual direction slots").ylab("Attempted slots");
  auto frontier=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(peaks,names).title("Actual peak pending").ylab("Coordinates on stack");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(attempts).add(frontier).size(1100,530).title("Equal grid cell counts; different selected regions and frontiers").fit_panels().save_svg("build/flood_fill_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| input | rows | cells | changed K | direction slots | in-bounds reads | peak pending |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Rectangle4 | 32 | 1024 | 1024 | 4096 | 3968 | 497 |
| Strip4 | 1 | 1024 | 1024 | 4096 | 2046 | 1 |
| Checker4 | 32 | 1024 | 1 | 4 | 2 | 1 |
| Checker8 | 32 | 1024 | 512 | 4096 | 3906 | 120 |
\image(figures/flood_fill_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Each input contains 1024 cells, but the selected regions and stack peaks differ. Direction slots equal four or eight times the changed count; panels have separate vertical scales.)
\alttext(Two native bar plots compare direction attempts and peak pending coordinates for a uniform rectangle, uniform strip, four-connected checkerboard and eight-connected checkerboard.)

`flood_fill_work` uses the ordinary mutation and records no history. An owned trace
also copies the entire input and output grids, even when K is small, and appends the
actual K pop records and D×K probe records. Its complete admission bound is

$$
C=2R+(D+3)N
$$
\alttext(The active trace upper bound is two times the row count plus D plus three times the total cell count.)

for an active fill, and C=2(R+N) for a no-op. The units are stored outer-row entries,
cell entries, visits and probes, not bytes or a peak-memory estimate. The default
one-million-entry cap is checked before copying; zero disables only the optional
cap. Overflow and container limits remain checked. The upper bound can reject a
large grid whose actual selected component is tiny. No truncated trace is returned.

> Input, ownership and replacement controls

Empty and zero-width rectangular grids return zero. A seed outside a nonempty grid
also returns zero. If the replacement already equals the seed value, zero means no
cells changed; it does not mean the seed has no component. A preexisting replacement
cell is not a bridge: [1,9,1] seeded at its left end becomes [9,9,1].

All rows must have the same width, including for empty-width, outside-seed and
same-value requests. Ragged grids throw before mutation; the original unchecked
implementation could read beyond a short row. Coordinates use guarded `size_t`
arithmetic. The pure neighborhood helper can check virtual full-width dimensions
without allocating an impossible grid or multiplying those dimensions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/flood_fill_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/label_images.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  using Grid=std::vector<std::vector<int>>;bool ragged=false,quota=false;
  Grid malformed{{1,1},{1}};const auto saved=malformed;
  try{dm::algorithms::flood_fill(malformed,0,1,9);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){ragged=true;}
  try{dm::algorithms::flood_fill_trace({{1,1},{1,1}},0,0,9,false,31);}catch(const std::length_error&){quota=true;}
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::flood_fill_trace({{1,1},{1,1}},0,0,9,false,32);
  Grid same{{1,1}},outside{{1}},barrier{{1,9,1}};const auto a=dm::algorithms::flood_fill(same,0,0,1),b=dm::algorithms::flood_fill(outside,1,0,9),c=dm::algorithms::flood_fill(barrier,0,0,9);
  std::print("Ragged rejected {}; unchanged {}; whole cap31 rejected {}; cap32 changed {}.\n",ragged,malformed==saved,quota,exact.work.filled);
  std::print("Same-value changed {}; outside-seed changed {}; [1,9,1] changed {}; output [{},{},{}].\n",a,b,c,barrier[0][0],barrier[0][1],barrier[0][2]);
  const auto full=std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max();const auto virtual_corner=dm::algorithms::flood_fill_neighborhood(full,full,full-1,full-1,true);
  std::print("Virtual size_t-max lower-right corner: NW {}; N {}; NE {}; W {}; E {}; SW {}; S {}; SE {}.\n",virtual_corner.candidates[0].in_bounds,virtual_corner.candidates[1].in_bounds,virtual_corner.candidates[2].in_bounds,virtual_corner.candidates[3].in_bounds,virtual_corner.candidates[4].in_bounds,virtual_corner.candidates[5].in_bounds,virtual_corner.candidates[6].in_bounds,virtual_corner.candidates[7].in_bounds);
  Grid input{{std::numeric_limits<int>::min(),std::numeric_limits<int>::min()},{0,std::numeric_limits<int>::min()}};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::flood_fill_trace(input,0,0,std::numeric_limits<int>::max());const auto original=trace.input;input[0][0]=7;
  std::print("Full signed values: target {}; replacement {}; changed {}; owned input unchanged {}.\n",trace.work.target,trace.work.new_value,trace.work.filled,trace.input==original&&trace.input[0][0]!=input[0][0]);
  dm::graphics::diagram_value_images({{trace.input,"Signed input"},{trace.output,"Signed replacement"}}).save_svg("build/flood_fill_controls.svg");
  if(!ragged||malformed!=saved||!quota||a||b||c!=1||trace.work.filled!=3)throw std::runtime_error("checked controls");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Ragged rejected true; unchanged true; whole cap31 rejected true; cap32 changed 4.
Same-value changed 0; outside-seed changed 0; [1,9,1] changed 1; output [9,9,1].
Virtual size_t-max lower-right corner: NW true; N true; NE false; W true; E false; SW false; S false; SE false.
Full signed values: target -2147483648; replacement 2147483647; changed 3; owned input unchanged true.
\image(figures/flood_fill_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Every int is a valid categorical value. Three INT_MIN cells become INT_MAX while zero remains unchanged; the trace owns both grids.)
\alttext(Two signed two-by-two grids print full integer values, showing three negative minimum integers replaced by positive maximum integers and one zero left white.)

The ordinary in-place API can retain earlier paint if an allocation or work-counter
failure interrupts an active traversal. Shape and domain validation precede any
mutation. The trace accepts read-only input and returns independent storage; a
failure returns no partial trace and never paints the caller's grid.

> Scope and reproducibility

This implementation uses exact equality and defaults to four connectivity. The
[scikit-image flood-fill API](https://scikit-image.org/docs/stable/api/skimage.segmentation.html#skimage.segmentation.flood_fill)
also offers tolerance and arbitrary footprints, and defaults to full connectivity
when neither footprint nor connectivity is supplied. Its
[worked example](https://scikit-image.org/docs/stable/auto_examples/segmentation/plot_floodfill.html)
shows why tolerance can matter for noisy intensities. Those options are outside this
integer exact-value API. The method here retains the original explicit cell stack;
no scanline-space or tolerance guarantees are implied.

Every displayed result and figure is generated by a complete editable C++ program.
The examples use native deterministic fixtures, with no external dataset. The core
is checked against frozen original final grids and actual traversal records, an
independent breadth-first membership oracle, full-width geometry controls, ownership
and quota tests, and strict address/undefined-behavior sanitizers. Native figures
are saved to local build files and copied into the report by the report runner.
